# The upstream pi commit that scripts/rlp-fork.patch applies to.
#
# An install pins this rather than tracking upstream's default branch. Tracking
# HEAD meant every install was a bet on whether upstream had moved the lines the
# patch touches — and by the time this file was written, it had: a fresh
# `curl | sh` failed at "patch did not apply cleanly" for everyone, because a
# `--depth 1` clone also has none of the blobs `git apply --3way` needs to
# recover. Pinning makes an install reproducible and makes the failure mode
# impossible.
#
# Moving to a newer upstream is `rlp update`'s job, deliberately: it merges,
# re-checks every RLP marker survived, rebuilds, and rolls back cleanly. That is
# a verified step someone chose, not a side effect of installing today rather
# than yesterday.
#
# Regenerate this file together with the patch — they are one fact in two
# places, and only in that pair does either mean anything:
#
#   cd fork/pi
#   BASE=$(git merge-base origin/main HEAD)
#   git diff "$BASE" HEAD > ../../scripts/rlp-fork.patch
#   git rev-parse "$BASE"  # put it below, keeping this header
#
8ce69e9d2b171d173fe4b6b2b6256f1f4411e69d
